 First, notice $Angle \ ACB \equiv 60$ degrees because the sum of the angles in a triangle is 180 degrees and $Angle \ ABC  \equiv 60$ degrees. On the other hand, $Angle \ ABC \equiv Angle \ A = 120$ degrees because the angles that are across a "Through the Middle of the Box" (that is, through a transversal to two parallel lines) interior angles on each one of the lines are congruent. Note that $Looking at Angle \ ACB$ we can find $Angle \ ACD  \equiv Angle \ BCA \equiv Angle \ A = 120^\circ$ because $Angle \ ABC \equiv Angle \ ACD$ as I mentioned before. This is because AC bisects the angle. 
Think of it this way. Imagine you are making one of those geometric proofs where you are given $angle \ A = 120^\circ$. Take the vertex point to the side of the angle and make an extra line for each possible angle you need to find (140-60, 120-40, 80-40, 80-20, and 60-20)but take care with the vertices always pointing to the interior of the angle, not the exterior. 

