response:
 It is a problem on sequences! We use the fact that for a geometric sequence with first term $a$ and common ratio $r$, and the sum of the first $n$ terms is given by $\sum_{k=1}^n ar^k=\frac{ar(1-r^n)}{1-r}$ for $-1<r<1$.
So.,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\frac {1}{4}\right)\frac{1}{2}^k
=\frac{1}{4}\frac{\frac{1}{2}(1-{\frac{1}{2}}^n)}{1-\frac{1}{2}}
&=\frac{63}{128}.
\end{align*}
Now we simplify.
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{8}(1-\frac{1}{2^n})
&=\frac{63}{128} \\
\Rightarrow1-\frac{1}{2^n}
&=\frac{63}{16}\\
\Rightarrow\frac{1}{2^n}
&=1-\frac{63}{16}\\
\Rightarrow\frac{1}{2^n}
&=\frac{-47}{16}\\
\Rightarrow\frac{1}{2^n}
&=-\frac{47}{16}
\end{align*}
Since $\frac{1}{r^n}>0$ for all $n\in\mathbb{Z}^+$ and $r\in{(-1,1)}$, the equation cannot be solved. $(x=-1.0625)$
<answer>
It is impossible for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric series to be $\frac{63}{128}$ with a first term of $\frac{1}{4}$ and a second term of $\frac{1}{8}$.
However, with first term the same (i.e. $\frac{1}{4}$) and second term $\frac{1}{42}$, the sum of the first $7$ terms of the geometric series is
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^n \frac{1}{4} \left( \frac{1}{42} \right)^{k-1}
&=\frac{1}{4}\frac{1-\frac{1}{(42)^7}}{1-\frac{1}{42}},\\
&=\frac{119,999,928}{3 \times 8 \times 11 \times 31 \times 91,393}\\
&\approx 0.983,\\
&\text{i.e. }\frac{119,999,928}{3 \times 8 \times 11 \times 31 \times 91,393}.
\end{align*}

 First, notice $a=\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $r=\dfrac{1}{2}$ since $\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{8}}{\dfrac{1}{4}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{16}}{\dfrac{1}{8}}=\dfrac{1}{2}$, so the $n^\text{th}$ term is:
\begin{gather*}
u_{n}=\dfrac{1}{4}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n-1}.
\end{gather*}
Also, we know the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric sequence is:
\begin{gather*}
S_{n}=\dfrac{a}{1-r}\times\left(1-\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n}\right).
\end{gather*}
Now, just apply tht formula and solve for $n$. <end think>
So to find the answer, we have $128S_{n}=36$ which is the same as $128\left(\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{4}}{1-\dfrac{1}{2}}\right)\times\left(1-\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n}\right)=36$. Which turns to $64\left(1-\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n}\right)=36$. Now distribute the six four and solve for $n$. Which is 5.

